James wrote:

 > Not really.  Part of the "write once, run
 > anywhere" guarantee was that the VM would remain
 > stable.  At least some early proposals for
 > generics insisted on the fact that they didn't
 > require any changes in the VM, for example.

I still don't think that "stable" guarantees
anything except "old classes will run in new VM
releases", not the opposite.

 > That's the case for Linux and Windows, more or
 > less.  It's probably acceptable for a client
 > machine which doesn't run anything critical, but
 > we validate very carefully every upgrade of our
 > servers, to ensure that our server code still does
 > exactly what it is supposed to.  (We've recently
 > started using Linux for some servers, but they
 > have the automatic upgrade feature turned off.)

While I can (barely) understand this level of
paranoia, unless "you" just have fallen in love with
doing hugely redundant effort, you either pick
vendors you can trust, and who understand how to do
SQA on their code much better than you do, or write
everything yourself up from bare metal (a viable
option if you stick to something like FORTH).

For the last three days, each day, for reasons
you've probably seen on the evening TV news, I've
gotten a "critical security update" for Microsoft
Windows XP Explorer V6.x.  Should I wait until local
SQA can analyze that update, without source code,
knowing that while I do so there's a huge and well
known hacker hole into my computer that installs a
keystroke logger, or should I just "do the right
thing" and install the update immediately?

The thing you want is called "regression testing",
and if you have it in place, validating a minor
point upgrade to the JRE should be fully automated
and shouldn't take even a day.

